"[1303.5164] Kernelet: High-Throughput GPU Kernel Executions with Dynamic Slicing and Scheduling (https://arxiv.org/abs/1303.5164)\nciteturn9view0 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://arxiv.org/abs/1303.5164\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 172\nL0: 【0†Search】 【1†Submit】 【2†Donate†info.arxiv.org】 【3†Log in】\nL1: \nL2: Search arXiv [Input: Search papers by title, author, abstract, or ID...] [Input] [Input]\nL3: \nL4: Press Enter to search · 【4†Advanced search】\nL5: \nL6: # Computer Science > Distributed, Parallel, and Cluster Computing\nL7: \nL8: [Submitted on 21 Mar 2013]\nL9: # Title:Kernelet: High-Throughput GPU Kernel Executions with Dynamic Slicing and Scheduling\nL10: \nL11: Authors:【5†Jianlong Zhong】, 【6†Bingsheng He】\nL12: \nL13: View a PDF of the paper titled Kernelet: High-Throughput GPU Kernel Executions with Dynamic Slicing and Scheduling, by Jianlong Zhong and 1 other authors\nL14: \nL15: 【7†View PDF】 【8†HTML (experimental)】\nL16: > Abstract:Graphics processors, or GPUs, have recently been widely used as accelerators in the shared environments such as clusters and clouds. In such shared environments, many kernels are submitted to GPUs from different users, and throughput is an important metric for performance and total ownership cost. Despite the recently improved runtime support for concurrent GPU kernel executions, the GPU can be severely underutilized, resulting in suboptimal throughput.\nL17: In this paper, we propose Kernelet, a runtime system with dynamic slicing and scheduling techniques to improve the throughput of concurrent kernel executions on the GPU. With slicing, Kernelet divides a GPU kernel into multiple sub-kernels (namely slices). Each slice has tunable occupancy to allow co-scheduling with other slices and to fully utilize the GPU resources. We develop a novel and effective Markov chain based performance model to guide the scheduling decision.\nL18: Our experimental results demonstrate up to 31.1% and 23.4% performance improvement on NVIDIA Tesla C2050 and GTX680 GPUs, respectively.\nL19: Subjects:  | Distributed, Parallel, and Cluster Computing (cs.DC)\nL20: ACM (Association of Computing Machinery Classification) classes:  | I.3.1; D.1.3; C.4\nL21: Cite as:  | 【9†arXiv:1303.5164】 [cs.DC]\nL22:    | (or 【10†arXiv:1303.5164v1】 [cs.DC] for this version)\nL23:    | 【11†https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.1303.5164†doi.org】 arXiv-issued DOI via DataCite\nL24: ## Submission history\nL25: \nL26: From: Jianlong Zhong [【12†view email】]\nL27: [v1] Thu, 21 Mar 2013 04:50:48 UTC (2,147 KB)\nL28: \nL29: Full-text links:\nL30: \nL31: ## Access Paper:\nL32: \nL33: View a PDF of the paper titled Kernelet: High-Throughput GPU Kernel Executions with Dynamic Slicing and Scheduling, by Jianlong Zhong and 1 other authors\nL34: \nL35:   * 【7†View PDF】\nL36:   * 【8†HTML (experimental)】\nL37:   * 【13†TeX Source】\nL38: \nL39: 【14†view license】\nL40: \nL41: ### Additional Features\nL42: \nL43:   * 【15†Audio Summary】\nL44: ### Current browse context:\nL45: \nL46: cs.DC\nL47: \nL48: 【16†< prev】   |   【17†next >】\nL49: \nL50: 【18†new】 | 【19†recent】 | 【20†2013-03】\nL51: \nL52: Change to browse by:\nL53: \nL54: 【21†cs】\nL55: \nL56: ### References & Citations\nL57: \nL58:   * 【22†SciX†scixplorer.org】\nL59:   * 【23†Google Scholar†scholar.google.com】\nL60:   * 【24†Semantic Scholar†api.semanticscholar.org】\nL61: ### 【25†DBLP†dblp.uni-trier.de】 - CS Bibliography\nL62: \nL63: 【26†listing†dblp.uni-trier.de】 | 【27†bibtex†dblp.uni-trier.de】\nL64: \nL65: 【28†Jianlong Zhong†dblp.uni-trier.de】\nL66: 【29†Bingsheng He†dblp.uni-trier.de】\nL67: \nL68: [Button: export BibTeX citation] Loading...\nL69: \nL70: ## BibTeX formatted citation\nL71: \nL72: [Button: ×]\nL73: \nL74: loading...\nL75: \nL76: Data provided by:\nL77: \nL78: ### Bookmark\nL79: \nL80: [Input] Bibliographic Tools\nL81: # Bibliographic and Citation Tools\nL82: \nL83: [Input] Bibliographic Explorer Toggle\nL84: \nL85: Biblio