h community collaborators\nL158: \nL159: arXivLabs is a framework that allows collaborators to develop and share new arXiv features directly on our website.\nL160: \nL161: Both individuals and organizations that work with arXivLabs have embraced and accepted our values of openness, community, excellence, and user data privacy. arXiv is committed to these values and only works with partners that adhere to them.\nL162: Have an idea for a project that will add value for arXiv's community? 【45†Learn more about arXivLabs†info.arxiv.org】.\nL163: \nL164: 【46†Which authors of this paper are endorsers?】 | Disable MathJax (【47†What is MathJax?†info.arxiv.org】)\nL165: \nL166: We gratefully acknowledge support from our major funders, 【48†member institutions†info.arxiv.org】, , and all contributors.\nL167: 【49†About†info.arxiv.org】 · 【50†Help†info.arxiv.org】 · 【51†Contact†info.arxiv.org】 · 【52†Subscribe†info.arxiv.org】 · 【53†Copyright†info.arxiv.org】 · 【54†Privacy†info.arxiv.org】 · 【55†Accessibility†info.arxiv.org】 · 【56†Operational Status (opens in new tab)†status.arxiv.org】\nL168: \nL169: Major funding support from\nL170: \nL171: 【57†(opens in new tab)†www.simonsfoundation.org】 【58†(opens in new tab)†www.sfi.org.bm】 【59†(opens in new tab)†www.schmidtsciences.org】\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n (https://arxiv.org/pdf/1303.5164)\nciteturn9view1 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: yesterday; Content type: application/pdf; Number of pages: 14; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://arxiv.org/pdf/1303.5164\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 1580\nL0@P0: arXiv:1303.5164v1 [cs.DC] 21 Mar 2013\nL1@P0: 1\nL2@P0: Kernelet: High-Throughput GPU Kernel\nL3@P0: Executions with Dynamic Slicing and Scheduling\nL4@P0: Jianlong Zhong, Bingsheng He\nL5@P0: Abstract—Graphics processors, or GPUs, have recently been widely used as accelerators in the shared environments such as clusters\nL6@P0: and clouds. In such shared environments, many kernels are submitted to GPUs from different users, and throughput is an important\nL7@P0: metric for performance and total ownership cost. Despite the recently improved runtime support for concurrent GPU kernel executions,\nL8@P0: the GPU can be severely underutilized, resulting in suboptimal throughput. In this paper, we propose Kernelet, a runtime system with\nL9@P0: dynamic slicing and scheduling techniques to improve the throughput of concurrent kernel executions on the GPU. With slicing, Kernelet\nL10@P0: divides a GPU kernel into multiple sub-kernels (namely slices). Each slice has tunable occupancy to allow co-scheduling with other\nL11@P0: slices and to fully utilize the GPU resources. We develop a novel and effective Markov chain based performance model to guide the\nL12@P0: scheduling decision. Our experimental results demonstrate up to 31.1% and 23.4% performance improvement on NVIDIA Tesla C2050\nL13@P0: and GTX680 GPUs, respectively.\nL14@P0: Index Terms—Graphics processors, Dynamic scheduling, Concurrent kernel executions, Dynamic Slicing, Performance models ✦\nL15@P0: 1 INTRODUCTION\nL16@P0: The graphics processing unit (or GPU) has become an ef\u0002fective accelerator for a wide range of applications from\nL17@P0: computation-intensive applications (e.g., [25], [26], [43])\nL18@P0: to data-intensive applications (e.g, [10], [18]). Compared\nL19@P0: with multicore CPUs, new-generation GPUs can have\nL20@P0: much higher computation power in terms of FLOPS\nL21@P0: and memory bandwidth. For example, an NVIDIA Tesla\nL22@P0: C2050 GPU can deliver the peak single precision float\u0002ing point performance of over one Tera FLOPS, and\nL23@P0: memory bandwidth of 144 GB/s. Due to their immense\nL24@P0: computation power and memory bandwidth, GPUs have\nL25@P0: been integrated into clusters and cloud computing in\u0002frastructures. In Top500 list of November 2012, two out\nL26@P0: of the top ten supercomputers are with GPUs integrated.\nL27@P0: Amazon and Penguin have provided virtual machines\nL28@P0: with GPUs. In both cluster and cloud environments,\nL29@P0: GPUs are often shared by many concurrent GPU pro\u0002grams (or kernels) (most likely submitted by multiple\nL30@P0: users). Additionally, to enable sharing GPUs remotely,\nL31@P0: a number of software frameworks such as rCUDA [7]\nL32@P0: and V-GPU [45] have been developed. This paper studies\nL33@P0: whether and how we can improve the throughput of\nL34@P0: concurrent kernel executions on the GPU.\nL35@P0: Throughput is an important optimization metric for\nL36@P0: efficiency and the total ownership cost of GPUs in such\nL37@P0: shared environments. First, many GPGPU applications\nL38@P0: such as scientific and financial computing tasks are usu\u0002ally throughput oriented [9]. A high throughput leads\nL39@P0: to the high performance and productivity for users.\nL40@P0: Second, compared with CPUs, GPUs are still expensive • J. Zhong and B. He are with School of Computer Engineering, Nanyang\nL41@P0: Technological University, Singapore, 639798.\nL42@P0: E-mail: jzhong2@ntu.edu.sg, bshe@ntu.edu.sg\nL43@P0: devices. Therefore, a high throughput not only means\nL44@P0: a high utilization on GPU resources but also the total\nL45@P0: ownership cost of running the application on the GPU.\nL46@P0: That might also be one of the reasons that GPUs are\nL47@P0: usually deployed and shared to handle many kernels\nL48@P0: from users.\nL49@P0: Recently, we have witnessed the success of GPGPU\nL50@P0: research. However, most studies focus on single-kernel\nL51@P0: optimizations (e.g., new data structures [17] and GPU\nL52@P0: friendly computing patterns [10], [11]). Despite the fruit-\nL53@P0: ful research, a single kernel usually severely under\u0002utilizes the GPU. This severe underutilization is mainly\nL54@P0: due to the inherent memory and computation behavior\nL55@P0: of a single kernel (e.g., irregular memory accesses and\nL56@P0: execution pipeline stalls). In our experiments, we have\nL57@P0: studied eight common kernels (details are presented\nL58@P0: in Section 5). On C2050, their average IPC is 0.52,\nL59@P0: which is far from the optimal value (1.0). Their memory\nL60@P0: bandwidth utilization is only ranging from 0.02% to 14%.\nL61@P0: Recent GPU architectures like NVIDIA Fermi [27] ar\u0002chitecture supports concurrent kernel executions, which\nL62@P0: allows multiple kernels to be executed on the GPU\nL63@P0: simultaneously if resources are allowed. In particular,\nL64@P0: Fermi adopts a form of cooperative kernel scheduling.\nL65@P0: Other kernels requesting the GPU must wait until the\nL66@P0: kernel occupying the GPU voluntarily yields control.\nL67@P0: Here, we use NVIDIA CUDA’s terminology, simply be\u0002cause CUDA is nowadays widely adopted in GPGPU\nL68@P0: applications. A kernel consists of multiple executions\nL69@P0: of thread blocks with the same program on different\nL70@P0: data, where the execution order of thread blocks is not\nL71@P0: defined. On the Fermi GPUs, one kernel can take the\nL72@P0: entire GPU as long as it has sufficient thread blocks\nL73@P0: to occupy all the multi-processors (even though it can\nL74@P0-1: have severely low resource utilization). Concurrent exe\u0002cution of two such kernels almost degrades to sequential2\nL75@P1: execution on individual kernels. Recent studies [34] on\nL76@P1: scheduling the concurrent kernels mainly focus on the\nL77@P1: kernels with low occupancy (i.e., the thread blocks of\nL78@P1: a single kernel cannot fully utilize all GPU multipro\u0002cessors). However, the occupancy of kernels (with large\nL79@P1: input data sizes in practice) is usually high after single\u0002kernel optimizations.\nL80@P1: Individual kernels as a whole cannot realize the real\nL81@P1: sharing of the GPU resources. A natural question is:\nL82@P1: can we slice the kernel into small pieces and then\nL83@P1: co-schedule slices from different kernels in order to\nL84@P1: improve the GPU resource utilization? The answer is\nL85@P1: yes. One observation is that GPU kernels (e.g., those\nL86@P1: written in CUDA or OpenCL) conform to the SPMD\nL87@P1: (Single Program Multiple Data) execution model. In\nL88@P1: such data-parallel executions, a kernel execution can\nL89@P1: usually be divided into multiple slices, each consisting\nL90@P1: of multiple thread blocks. Slices can be viewed as low\u0002occupancy kernels and can be executed simultaneously\nL91@P1: with slices from other kernels. The GPGPU concurrent\nL92@P1: kernel scheduling problem is thus converted to the slice\nL93@P1: scheduling problem.\nL94@P1: With slicing, we have two more issues to address. The\nL95@P1: first issue is on the slicing itself: what is the suitable\nL96@P1: slice size? How to perform the slicing in a transparent\nL97@P1: manner? The smallest granularity of a slice is one thread\nL98@P1: block, which can lead to significant runtime overhead of\nL99@P1: submitting too many such small slices onto the GPUs for\nL100@P1: execution. To the other extreme, the largest granularity\nL101@P1: of the slice is the entire kernel, which degrades to the\nL102@P1: non-sliced execution. The second issue is how to select\nL103@P1: the slices for co-schedule in order to maximize the GPU\nL104@P1: utilization.\nL105@P1: To address those issues, we develop Kernelet, a runtime\nL106@P1: system with dynamic slicing and scheduling techniques\nL107@P1: to improve the GPU utilization. Targeting at the concur\u0002rent kernel executions in the shared GPU environment,\nL108@P1: Kernelet dynamically performs slicing on the kernels,\nL109@P1: and the slices are carefully designed with tunable oc\u0002cupancy to allow slices from other kernels to utilize the\nL110@P1: GPU resources in a complementary way. For example,\nL111@P1: one slice utilizes the computation units and the other\nL112@P1: one on memory bandwidth. We develop a novel and ef\u0002fective Markov chain based performance model to guide\nL113@P1: kernel slicing and scheduling in order to maximize the\nL114@P1: GPU resource utilization. Compared with existing GPU\nL115@P1: performance models which are limited to a single kernel\nL116@P1: only, our model are designed to handle heterogeneous\nL117@P1: workloads (i.e., slices from different kernels). We further\nL118@P1: develop a greedy co-scheduling algorithm to always co\u0002schedule the slices from the two kernels with the highest\nL119@P1: performance gain according to our performance model.\nL120@P1: We have evaluated Kernelet on two latest GPU ar\u0002chitectures (Tesla C2050 and GTX680). The GPU kernels\nL121@P1: under study have different memory and computational\nL122@P1: characteristics. Experimental results show that 1) our an\u0002alytical model can accurately capture the performance of\nL123@P1: heterogeneous workloads on the GPU, 2) our scheduling\nL124@P1: increase the GPU throughput by up to 31.1% and 23.4%\nL125@P1: on C2050 and GTX680, respectively.\nL126@P1: Organization. The rest of the paper is organized as\nL127@P1: follows. We introduce the background and definition of\nL128@P1: our problem in Section 2. Section 3 presents the system\nL129@P1: overview, followed by detailed design and implementa\u0002tion in Section 4. The experimental results are presented\nL130@P1: in Section 5. We review the related work in Section 6 and\nL131@P1: conclude this paper in Section 7.\nL132@P1: 2 BACKGROUND AND PROBLEM DEFINITION\nL133@P1: In this section, we briefly introduce the background\nL134@P1: on GPU architectures, and next present our problem\nL135@P1: definition.\nL136@P1: 2.1 GPU Architectures\nL137@P1: GPUs have rapidly evolved into a powerful accel\u0002erator for many applications, especially after CUDA\nL138@P1: was released by NVIDIA [30]. The top tags in\nL139@P1: http://gpgpu.org/ show that a wide range of appli\u0002cations have been accelerated with GPGPU techniques,\nL140@P1: including vision and graphics, image processing, linear\nL141@P1: algebra, molecular dynamics, physics simulation and\nL142@P1: scientific computing etc. Those applications cover quite\nL143@P1: a wide range of computation and memory intensiveness.\nL144@P1: In the shared environment like clusters and cloud, it\nL145@P1: is very likely that users submit kernels from different\nL146@P1: applications to the same GPU. Thus, it is feasible to\nL147@P1: schedule kernels with different memory and computa\u0002tion characteristics to better utilize GPU resources.\nL148@P1: This paper focuses on the design and implementation\nL149@P1: with NVIDIA CUDA. Since OpenCL and CUDA have\nL150@P1: very similar designs, our design and implementation\nL151@P1: can be extended to OpenCL with little modification.\nL152@P1: Kernelet takes advantage of concurrent kernel execution\nL153@P1: capability of new-generation GPUs like NVIDIA Fermi\nL154@P1: GPUs. With the introduction of CUDA, a GPU can be\nL155@P1: viewed as a many-core processor with a set of streaming\nL156@P1: multi-processors (SM). Each SM has a set of scalar cores,\nL157@P1: which executes the instructions in the SIMD (Single\nL158@P1: Instruction Multiple Data) manner. The SMs are in turn\nL159@P1: executed in the SPMD manner. The program is called\nL160@P1: kernel.\nL161@P1: In CUDA’s abstraction, GPU threads are organized in a\nL162@P1: hierarchical configuration: usually 32 threads are firstly\nL163@P1: grouped into a warp; warps are further grouped into\nL164@P1: thread blocks. The CUDA runtime performs mapping\nL165@P1: and scheduling at the granularity of thread blocks. Each\nL166@P1: thread block is mapped and scheduled on an SM, and\nL167@P1: cannot be split among multiple SMs. Once a thread block\nL168@P1: is scheduled, its warps become active on the SM. Warp is\nL169@P1: the smallest scheduling unit on the GPU. Each SM uses\nL170@P1: one or more warp schedulers to issue instructions of the\nL171@P1: active warps. Another important resource of the GPU\nL172@P1: is shared memory, which is a small piece of scratchpad\nL173@P1-2: memory at the scope of a thread block. It is small and3\nL174@P2: User N\nL175@P2: (VM N)\nL176@P2: ĂĂ\nL177@P2: ĂĂ\nL178@P2: Nvidia GPUs\nL179@P2: GPU Virtualization Layer\nL180@P2: User 1\nL181@P2: (VM 1)\nL182@P2: GPU Server\nL183@P2: ĂĂ\nL184@P2: Kernelet\nL185@P2: (a) GPU shared within one ma\u0002chine.\nL186@P2: ĂĂ\nL187@P2: Nvidia GPUs\nL188@P2: ĂĂ\nL189@P2: GPU Server\nL190@P2: ĂĂ network\nL191@P2: Remote\nL192@P2: Node 1\nL193@P2: API Inception API Inception\nL194@P2: Remote\nL195@P2: Node N\nL196@P2: Kernelet\nL197@P2: API reception framwork\nL198@P2: (like rCUDA)\nL199@P2: (b) GPU shared by remote\nL200@P2: clients.\nL201@P2: Fig. 1: Application scenarios of concurrent kernel execu\u0002tions on the GPUs.\nL202@P2: has very low latency. Shared memory is visible for all\nL203@P2: the threads in the same thread block.\nL204@P2: We define the SM occupancy as the ratio of active\nL205@P2: warps to the maximum active warps that are allowed to\nL206@P2: run on the SM. Higher occupancy means higher thread\nL207@P2: parallelism. The aggregated register and shared memory\nL208@P2: usage of all warps should not exceed the total amount\nL209@P2: of available registers and shared memory on an SM.\nL210@P2: Block Scheduling. CUDA runtime system maps\nL211@P2: thread blocks to SMs in a round-robin manner. If the\nL212@P2: number of thread blocks in a kernel is less than the\nL213@P2: number of SMs on the GPU, each thread block will be\nL214@P2: executed on a dedicated SM; otherwise, multiple thread\nL215@P2: blocks will be mapped to the same SM. The number of\nL216@P2: thread blocks that can be concurrently executed on the SM\nL217@P2: depends on their total resource requirements (in terms\nL218@P2: of registers and shared memory). If the kernel currently\nL219@P2: being executed on the GPU cannot fully utilize the GPU,\nL220@P2: the GPU allows to schedule thread blocks from other\nL221@P2: kernels for execution.\nL222@P2: GPU Code Compilation. In the process of CUDA\nL223@P2: compilation, the compiler first compiles the CUDA C\nL224@P2: code to PTX (Parallel Thread Execution) code. PTX is\nL225@P2: a virtual machine assembly language and offers a sta\u0002ble programming model for evolving hardware archi\u0002tectures. PTX code is further compiled to native GPU\nL226@P2: instructions (SASS). The GPU can only execute the SASS\nL227@P2: code. CUDA executables and libraries usually provide\nL228@P2: either PTX code or SASS code or both. In the shared\nL229@P2: environments, the source code is usually not available\nL230@P2: for kernel scheduling upon users submit the kernels.\nL231@P2: Thus, Kernelet should be designed to work on both PTX\nL232@P2: and SASS code.\nL233@P2: 2.2 Problem Definition\nL234@P2: Application scenario. We consider two typical applica\u0002tion scenarios in the shared environments as shown in\nL235@P2: Figure 1. One is sharing the GPUs among multiple ten\u0002ants in the virtualized environment (e.g., cloud). As illus\u0002trated in Figure 1a, there is usually a GPU virtualization\nL236@P2: layer integrated with the hypervisor. Figure 1b shows the\nL237@P2: other scenario, in which GPU servers offer API reception\nL238@P2: softwares (like rCUDA [7], [45]) to support local/remote\nL239@P2: CUDA kernel launches. In both scenarios, the GPU faces\nL240@P2: multiple pending kernel launch requests. Kernelet can be\nL241@P2: applied to schedule those pending kernels.\nL242@P2: Our study mainly considers the throughput issues of\nL243@P2: sharing a single GPU. Kernelet can be extended to multi\u0002ple GPUs with a workload dispatcher to each individual\nL244@P2: GPU.\nL245@P2: We have made the following assumptions on the\nL246@P2: kernels.\nL247@P2: 1. We target at the concurrent kernel executions on\nL248@P2: the shared GPU. The kernels are usually through\u0002put oriented, with flexibility on the response time\nL249@P2: for scheduling. Still, we do not assume the a priori\nL250@P2: knowledge of the order of the kernel arrival.\nL251@P2: 2. Thread blocks in a kernel are independent with\nL252@P2: each other. This assumption is mostly true for the\nL253@P2: GPGPU kernels due to SPMD programming model.\nL254@P2: Most kernels in NVIDIA SDK and benchmarks like\nL255@P2: Parboil [13] do not have dependency among thread\nL256@P2: blocks in the same kernel. This assumption ensures\nL257@P2: that our slicing technique on a given kernel is safe.\nL258@P2: The data dependency among thread blocks can be\nL259@P2: identified with standard static or dynamic program\nL260@P2: analysis.\nL261@P2: We formally define the terminology in Kernelet.\nL262@P2: Kernel. A kernel K consists of k thread blocks with\nL263@P2: IDs, 0, 1, 2, ..., (k − 1).\nL264@P2: Slice. A slice is a subset of the thread blocks of a\nL265@P2: launched kernel. Block IDs of a slice is continuous in\nL266@P2: the grid index space. The size of a slice s is defined as\nL267@P2: the number of thread blocks contained in the slice.\nL268@P2: Slicing plan. Given a kernel K, a slicing plan S(K) is\nL269@P2: a scheme slicing K into a sequence of n slices (s0, s1, s2,\nL270@P2: ..., sn−1). We denote the slicing plan to be K=s0, s1, s2,\nL271@P2: ..., sn−1.\nL272@P2: Co-schedule. Co-schedule cs defines concurrent exe\u0002cution of n (n ≥ 1) slices, denoted as s0, ..., sn−1. All the\nL273@P2: n slices are active on the GPU.\nL274@P2: Scheduling plan. Given a set of n kernels K0, K1, ...,\nL275@P2: Kn, a scheduling plan C (cs0, cs1, ..., csn−1) determines\nL276@P2: a sequence of co-schedules in their order of execution.\nL277@P2: csi is launched before csj if i < j. All thread blocks of\nL278@P2: the n kernels occur in one of the co-schedules once and\nL279@P2: only once. A scheduling plan embodies a slicing plan for\nL280@P2: each kernel.\nL281@P2: We define the performance benefit of co-scheduling n\nL282@P2: kernels to be the co-scheduling profit (CP) in Eq. (1).\nL283@P2: IP Ci and cIP Ci are IPC (Instruction Per Cycle) for\nL284@P2: sequential execution and concurrent execution of kernel i\nL285@P2: respectively. Our definition is similar to those in the pre\u0002vious studies on CPU multi-threaded co-scheduling [20],\nL286@P2: [39].\nL287@P2: CP = 1 −\nL288@P2: 1\nL289@P2: Pn\nL290@P2: i=1\nL291@P2: cIP Ci\nL292@P2: IP Ci\nL293@P2-3: (1)4\nL294@P3: Problem definition. Given a set of kernels for execu\u0002tion, the problem is to determine the optimal scheduling\nL295@P3: plan (and slicing) so that the total execution time for\nL296@P3: those kernels is minimized. That corresponds to the\nL297@P3: maximized throughput. Given a set of n kernels K0,\nL298@P3: K1, ..., Kn−1, we aim at finding the optimal scheduling\nL299@P3: plan C for a minimized total execution time of C(S0(K0),\nL300@P3: S1(K1), ..., Sn−1(Kn−1)), or maximized co-scheduling\nL301@P3: profit. Note, in the shared GPU environment, the arrival\nL302@P3: of new kernels trigger the recalculation of the optimiza\u0002tion on the kernel residuals and new kernels.\nL303@P3: 3 SYSTEM OVERVIEW\nL304@P3: In this section, we present the rationales on the Kernelet\nL305@P3: design, followed by an overview of the Kernelet runtime.\nL306@P3: 3.1 Design Rationale\nL307@P3: Since kernels are submitted in an ad-hoc manner in our\nL308@P3: application scenarios, the scheduling decision has to be\nL309@P3: made at real time. The optimization process should take\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nInternal Error ()\nciteturn9view2 [wordlim: 200] Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://github.com/ingonyama-zk/icicle/tree/6c77775\",\"lineno\":null}); \nFailed to fetch https://github.com/ingonyama-zk/icicle/tree/6c77775: Cache miss"
